# XSH setvbuf()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.setvbuf.001
anchor     functions/setvbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       For each of _IOFBF, _IOLBF and _IONBF, with a buffer of the caller's
           and with a null buffer, a new stream that setvbuf() accepts (a
           refusal is allowed and noted) carries 1000 bytes written with
           fputc() and fputs() to the file intact by fclose().
nomutant   Data arriving intact is the library's ordinary behaviour; a
           fault that broke it would break every stdio test, and the
           mode-specific faults are caught by 003 to 006.
test       t/001-modes.c

assertion  XSH.setvbuf.002
anchor     functions/setvbuf.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       setvbuf() on a new stream with a type that is none of _IOFBF, _IOLBF
           and _IONBF returns a non-zero value.
test       t/002-bad-type.c

assertion  XSH.setvbuf.003
anchor     functions/setvbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      1988
says       On a stream set _IONBF, after fputc('a') and fputs("bc") (no fflush()),
           the file holds "abc".
note       disputed (Q-33): setvbuf() says each mode shall cause that
           buffering, but XSH 2.5 and ISO C make support for the
           buffering characteristics implementation-defined; a FAIL is
           logged as INSPECT.
test       t/003-unbuffered.c

assertion  XSH.setvbuf.004
anchor     functions/setvbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      1988
says       On a stream set _IOLBF (with a 256-byte buffer), after fputs("ab\n")
           (no fflush()), the file holds "ab\n".
note       disputed (Q-33): setvbuf() says each mode shall cause that
           buffering, but XSH 2.5 and ISO C make support for the
           buffering characteristics implementation-defined; a FAIL is
           logged as INSPECT.
test       t/004-line.c

assertion  XSH.setvbuf.005
anchor     functions/setvbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With setvbuf(f, g + 16, _IOFBF, 16) on a 64-byte array g filled with
           '#' (accepted), after 100 bytes are written and the stream is
           closed, g[0..15] and g[32..63] are still '#', and the file holds
           the 100 bytes.
test       t/005-own-buffer-size.c

assertion  XSH.setvbuf.006
anchor     functions/setvbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      1988
says       On a new stream, after setvbuf() with an invalid type fails,
           setvbuf(_IONBF) returns 0 and fputc('q') then reaches the file
           without a flush.
note       disputed (Q-33): setvbuf() says each mode shall cause that
           buffering, but XSH 2.5 and ISO C make support for the
           buffering characteristics implementation-defined; a FAIL is
           logged as INSPECT.
test       t/006-after-failed-call.c
